<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melqart</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melqart was the chief god of the Phoenician city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, whose name means 'king of the city'. Worshipped as protector of the city and its colonies, he had an annual festival of awakening, and his cult spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and Gades in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, and coins of Kition and other cities show the god as Heracles-Melqart.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melqart.jpg" alt="Melqart" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Testa_di_herakles-melqart,_500-480_ac_ca,_da_cipro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Melqart is written MLQRT in the Phoenician abjad. Edward Lipinski theorises that it derives from MLK QRT, meaning 'King of the City'; in Akkadian it appears as Milqartu. The earliest occurrence of the name is in the 9th-century BCE Ben-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> inscription, found in 1939 north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, erected by the son of the king of Aram 'for his lord Melqart'. Archaeological evidence for the cult is earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, and it seems to have spread westward with the colonies Tyre founded, eventually overshadowing the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, following Menander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, says that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiram-i">Hiram I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> (c. 965-935 BCE) built the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and was the first to celebrate the awakening (egersis) of Heracles in the month Peritius. This annual awakening may identify Melqart as a dying-and-rising god, and he was central to the Phoenician spring festival during which he died and was resurrected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>According to María Eugenia Aubet, the name of Melqart was invoked in oaths sanctioning contracts, and it was customary to build a temple to him, as protector of Tyrian traders, in each new colony. At Cádiz the temple is as early as the earliest vestiges of Phoenician occupation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> sent a yearly tribute of 10% of its public treasury to the god in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, and in Tyre the high priest of Melqart ranked second only to the king. Temples are known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at Cádiz, Ibiza and Cartagena, and at Lixus on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><p>The Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, just before setting off for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, made a pilgrimage to Gades, the most ancient seat of Phoenician worship in the west, and prayed and sacrificed at the Altar of Melqart. On his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">New Carthage</a> he saw a vision that he believed Melqart had sent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified Melqart with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and Hercules, distinguishing him as the Tyrian Hercules. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> he was syncretised with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a>, and from the sixth century BCE he was often shown with the lion skin and club of Heracles. Herodotus, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, describes a richly adorned temple with two pillars, one of pure gold and the other of smaragdos, and reports the priests' claim that it was as old as the city.</p><p>Because evidence is sparse, the nature of his cult is debated. William F. Albright suggested he was an underworld god, partly through the god Milku, who may be Melqart and is sometimes equated with Nergal; others regard this as coincidence. It has also been proposed that he began as a sea god who gained solar attributes, or the reverse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melqart” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the later account of Philo of Byblos, said to come from Sanchuniathon of Berytus, the creator Elion was father of the gods and married Beruth, “the city”, as other gods were linked with theirs: <b>Melqart</b> with Tyre, Chemosh with Moab, Tanit and Baal Hammon with Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the key inscriptions are the sarcophagi of Ahiram, Tabnit and Eshmunazar II, the Bodashtart inscriptions and the Temple of Eshmun, the Kilamuwa Stela, the Karatepe bilingual, the Çineköy inscription, the Nora Stone, the Mdina Steles, the Cippi of <b>Melqart</b> and the Pyrgi Tablets.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Iberian religion was polytheistic and shaped by Greek, Carthaginian and Phoenician practice, and Tanit, Baal, <b>Melqart</b>, Artemis, Demeter and Asclepius were known and worshipped in the region.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Tyre honoured <b>Melqart</b>, “king of the city”, whose “awakening” was celebrated each year at a festival that, according to Josephus, Hiram I was the first to hold.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In Canaan, as Egyptian power declined in the Iron Age, peoples and cities gathered round patron gods: Chemosh of Moab, Milcom of Ammon, Qos of Edom, Yahweh of Israel, <b>Melqart</b> of Tyre and Eshmun of Sidon.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>Gadir was governed by its own elite under institutions modelled on Tyre, and its temple of <b>Melqart</b>, later identified with Hercules, drew pilgrims from across the Mediterranean; Hannibal visited before marching on Italy in 218 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/gadir">Gadir</a></li></ul></section>
